er would sing and Mrs A played the piano. Mother enjoyed reading and read them the classics. Mrs A has happy memories of long family walks, staying with relatives at Burcot and outings on a houseboat to Newnham. Her father was a keen cricket spectator. From the age of five she acted in village plays and went to the theatre to see Gilbert and Sullivan and music hall at the Old Palace.	Her mother was not very strong and used to have terrible bilious attacks and bad periods. Mother would make her own cough remedies.	Extensive information on running of household and childrearing and class.	Interviewee's home	2	Oxford	6	~	~	 	C707/23/1-2	1	1	81	2000int023	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	2	Open reel	1	2	1	1
24	1	Mr	J.S.	Adams	A	1882	1	4	0	4	1	1910	2	1837	1893	999	1914	1	5	97	3	3	0	0	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	1	4	4	4	4	9	9	9	9	9	9	4	4	4	4	1	1	9	9	9	4	9	9	9	21	99	99	2	3	3	9	2	6	3	2	6	3	9	2	6	3	0	9	9	2	9	4	0	7	7	9	999	999	999	2	5	7	7	1	9	9	9	9	24	63	63	63	2	1	2	2	2	2	9	4	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	4	2	1	98	98	4	3	2	2	9	4	0	1	2	3	1	999	9	9	2	99	7	5	999	9	9	2	99	3	3	999	9	9	2	99	24	2	1	4	4	4	2	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	99	99	99	9	9	9	9	9	Pottery manager	B	2	2	5	Family, housing, discipline, education, work, leisure, religion, politics, marriage, childbirth and local community.	Youngest of 4 children. Two sisters and a brother and saw himself as an unwanted child.	Married in 1910 in the Methodist chapel with a guard of honour from the Boy's Brigade and went to Blackpool for the honeymoon. They had two children. He met his wife at church. Father in law, a former slip maker was the first Doctor of Music in the Potteries	Born in Normacott, moved to Stoke then back to Normacott where family owned two neighbouring terraced houses (and one in Stoke) built for her father one of which was left to Mr A. On marriage, they rented a two bed house and later bought their own house.	Started at St Peter's School, Stoke but 'not scholastically inclined'. Father had contemplated his going on to high school but he died at that time and so he went to Uttoxeter Board School until 14.	His father was a dipper and oven foreman which was very skilled work. He was on Longton Town Council but died in 1893. (Information on pottery processes). Mother was a dressmaker before marriage and lived on the rent from the three houses when husband died.	Started as a warehouse boy at Ainsley's, a premier china factory, but changed jobs and became a clerk at Co-operative Wholesale Society depot and a member of the Clerk's Union. He was in the army for six months and then worked his way up in the Co-operative Wholesale Society to management, joining the British Pottery Managers Union. His wife was a teacher before marriage and later did home teaching for a child with polio	Father was a Liberal, a Gladstonian man, and Mr Adams was also a liberal who still took the Liberal News. He enjoyed going to political meetings and recalls the visit of Lloyd George at Victoria Hall in 1910.	Family were Primitive Methodist where father was a lay preacher. There is a marble slab to his memory in one of the Normacott chapels. Occasionally they had prayers at home 'when father felt moved to such a thing'. Grace at meals.	Father enjoyed gardening. Annual holidays to Blackpool and Sunday School outings. Joined the Boy's Brigade and enjoyed cycling and football. He would accompany his brother to Stoke City Football Club matches. The family would entertain friends who all worked in the potteries. His father read the Sentinal and Mr Adams later took the daily News and the News Chronicle.	~	Extensive information on running of household and childrearing and class.	Interviewee's home	2	Stoke-on-Trent	8	~	Elizabeth Sloan	 	C707/24/1	1	1	56	2000int024	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	1	Open reel	1	2	1	1
25	1	Mr	J.S.	Hine	H	1896	1	4	0	6	1	1924	1	1857	1906	999	999	1	5	97	2	2	0	0	3	3	0	0	9	9	9	9	9	9	1	4	4	4	1	4	4	4	4	4	4	4	4	4	1	9	1	9	2	9	3	6	9	1	2	99	99	9	2	5	2	2	6	3	9	99	9	9	2	6	3	0	9	9	5	9	3	0	6	0	1	6	999	999	2	1	7	3	1	9	9	9	1	25	63	63	999	9	9	1	2	1	2	7	9	3	2	7	9	9	9	3	9	9	9	3	4	9	99	99	2	3	3	2	9	4	0	2	4	3	4	999	9	9	9	99	9	9	999	9	9	9	99	5	2	999	9	9	9	99	25	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	99	99	99	9	9	9	9	9	Builders labourer	F	6	6	9	Family, housing, discipline, education, work, leisure, religion, politics, marriage, childbirth and local community.	Seven children. Sixth in birth order in a very poor family due to father's drunkenness and early death. Mother was very affectionate and helpful to neighbours - she would lay out people in death without payment. She was a very independent woman with the heart of a lion.	1924	Born in Stoke-on-Trent in two up two down house with outside toilet and coal house. Moved to a three bed house in Mount Pleasant, then another cheaper house where he has lived ever since.	Mother could read but only learnt to write aged 50. He went to Stoke St Peter's Junior school and on to the Senior School, leaving at standard 6.	Grandfather was a master crate maker, his father, a foreman stone mason for the largest building contractor in the Potteries who earned extra money carving grave stones. He died aged 49. His mother did office cleaning and took in washing.	Started as an errand boy in a draper's shop, followed by a shoe shop. He worked in a pot bank in a biscuit warehouse (info on pottery processes) then went down the pit (info on coalmining). He joined the army and after 1919 became a labourer for the Stoke Gas Works, returning to the pit then doing other casual work. He became an oven drawer then took a permanent job as a marler later as a building labourer where he joined a Trade Union.	Father voted Liberal but not really interested in politics. JH went to political meetings at Victoria Hall and heard Bevin, J.H. Thomas and Philip Snowden.	Church of England at Stoke St Peter's where he attended Sunday School, Bible class and evening service. Mother was in the Mothers Union and the parson would call on her weekly.	Family always entertained family and friends. They would go to music hall at the Hippodrome and to the pantomime on Boxing Day. His mother read the Liverpool Weekly Post. JH went on Sunday School treats on barge to Trentham park, cycled and played cricket and football, following Stoke City Football Club.. He used to go camping with the Boys Brigade, played the bugle and went to their meetings four times a week. The family had a HMV phonogram with a wax cylinder. He read avidly and joined the local library.	His father, who was a drunkard walked into a pool in Trentham Park and died of pneumonia. JH, as a young lad, had an operation for appendicitis at Royal Infirmary. He smashed his kneecap on a building site but received compensation via his Union. Mother died of cancer when he was 21.	Extensive information on running of household and childrearing and class. JH commented that, at the time, you never thought about class at all.	Interviewee's home	2	Stoke-on-Trent	8	13.09.1968	Elizabeth Sloan	 	C707/25/1-2	1	1	108	2000int025	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	2	Open reel	1	2	1	1
