Roman Catholic church provided hot food for collection at difficult times. She went to Roman Catholic Sunday school only occasionally as had no clothes but later gave up religion when married. Mother refused to go to Protestant church.	Father was very musical and played the accordion while mother sang. No books or magazines in the house. Father read the Daily Herald. Parents never went out. She went to magic lantern shows and played dominoes and cards. Once working, went to Adelphi Cinema and for walks to New Broughton.	Brother died of TB.	Extensive information on running of household, childrearing and social class. 'Nightman' would visit houses to see if there were overcrowding, investigating sleeping arrangements. Orange celebrations on July 12. March of Roman Catholic Foresters on 17th March. Reference to George Wise as a trouble maker. Recalls how Tom Mann had Roman Catholics and Protestants marching together in Shiel Park with green and orange ribbons in lapels. Recalls being lost and taken to sheltering home. People would borrow special set of sheets for lying out, then launder and return them. Description of a wake.	Interviewee's home	3	Liverpool	5	??.03.1970	Ray Collins	 	C707/101/1-3	1	1	66	2000int101	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	3	Open reel	1	2	1	1
