rked shovelling coal at a rubber works at four pence an hour. Stayed there for 45 years as trimmer and then a stoker in boiler house. Often worked 84 hours a week, sometimes 90 including Sunday. Wife had worked at fancy box making.	No idea of father's politics. WW had no interest in politics.	Went to St Matthews’s Church three times on Sunday as in choir. Father never went to church. After marriage continued to go with his wife.	No books in house. Father read Saturday Express and spent free time in pub. Never taken out visiting or on outings. No holidays just Sunday school outings on barge along canal. Some friends visited stepmother who was Welsh from Wales. Later joined Boys Brigade and went cycling. When married played organ at home. They rarely went out except to Prestatyn on Bank Holidays. Visits from wife's mother. WW had no friends.	Father sometimes unable to work due to complaint with his hands.	Extensive information on running of household, childrearing and social class. Funeral horses had white reins for a child. Poor children got clothes from police and families would get a 4 pound loaf, tea, sugar and oatmeal from depots.	Interviewee's home	3	Liverpool	5	??.03.1970	~	 	C707/103/1-2	1	1	63	2000int103	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	2	Open reel	1	2	1	1
