e. Trip to Skegness. Enjoyed walking, dancing, musical comedy, and the cinema. Heard Gracie Fields. Belonged to the Library. Husband played football and billiards.	Two children died in infancy. AW's husband lost an eye in World War I. One of AW's daughters had pneumonia the other bronchitis.	Tried to leave home aged 13. Borrowed books from school. AW won first prize for reciting. Children taught parents to read. Family had a Yorkshire terrier as a pet. Mother had to go for support from Board of Guardians. Mother paid into boot club, clothing club, and insurance. AW belonged to savings club and sick club. Husband helped in the house. Extensive, anecdotal interview. Details: father's work; chebonning; second-hand shoes; Bostock and Rumbolds; coronation celebrations; childhood; mother's medicines; lace industry; social class; Nottingham dialect; hospital; unemployment; British Legion; doctors.	Interviewee's home	2	Nottingham	1	02.02.1971	Jean Jacobs	 	C707/198/1-4	1	1	144	2000int198	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	2	Open reel	2	2	1	1
