rch organist. Children had birthday parties. Father had technical books, children had own books. Family took the local Newcastle and South Shields newspapers. Parents had good friends and neighbours. Visited relatives in North Seaton. Family and friends had holidays in rented cottages in Catton and Arondale. Sunday school outings to Leamside. Belonged to the Church Lads Brigade. Brothers went on father's ships to Hamburg and Rotterdam, aged 6-7 years. Father played football for North Alsea. Attended Sunday school concerts at the Temperance Hall South Shields. Hobbies included stamp and butterfly collecting; skeletonising leaves; walking with school friends; playing cricket. Later played tennis, golf and hockey. Played on South Shields hockey team and Westow Lawn Tennis team. Coastal walks with friends. Variety shows at the Empire.	Mother semi-invalid. Father blind in later life.	Family had live-in domestic help, became the family's housekeeper after mother's death. Mother baked bread. Mother paid into thrift club. Family considered themselves lower middle-class. Landlord reluctant to install electricity. Father lived with GHE in Horden. Father was killed in an air raid. Detail: social class; occupations.	Interviewee's home	2	South Shields	4	27.10.1970; 02.11.1970	Ian Stewart	 	C707/223/1-2	1	1	57	2000int223	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	2	Open reel	1	2	1	1
