an work. FHS's children went to Sunday school.	Father was a musician, played the concertina, played piano and banjo, and sang for parties and concert parties. Father took the Stand magazine, Daily Mail, Lloyds Weekly News. Visited paternal grandmother regularly. Family's friends and neighbours visited, played cards and made music. Went walking with father. Collected cigarette cards. Went on Band of Hope outings to Ashstead Woods, Burnham Beeches or Epsom Downs. Belonged to Livingstone Lodge. Belonged to Price's swimming club. After marriage, enjoyed reading, belonged to Price's Library, and enjoyed music. Took The People, Tit-Bits, Answers, Girls Friend, Boys Best Friend. Took wife to cinema once a week. Days out to Clapham Common and Wimbledon Common. Took wife and children to the seaside. Watched Chelsea on Saturdays. Played on whist drives. Belonged to National Sunday League.	Had pleurisy and pneumonia.	Family depended on charity organisation society. Father belonged to slate club. Clothes were mostly second-hand. Shoes. Children had tasks in the house. Father kept rabbits for food. Young brother drowned in the Thames. After marriage, FHS paid into thrift and loan club. Helped wife, decorated home, made children's toys. Comprehensive interview. Many anecdotes. Detail: family meals; family routine; school and school teacher, Mr Hutchins; pub life; police; Battersea; Manley Circus; siblings' careers.	Interviewee's home	3	London	3	04.03.1971	Trevor Lummis	 	C707/225/1-3	1	1	122	2000int225	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	3	Open reel	1	2	1	1
