b on Sundays. Mother went to Fellowship once a week. Christmas was made special. Family owned a harmonium. Family sang and played games. Books were Sunday school prizes. Family took the Sentinel, Daily Mirror or Daily Sketch and had comics given by friends. Enjoyed knitting. After leaving school, liked to go to the cinema.	Husband was certified invalid aged 19 years after injury at flint mill. ET contracted Scotch itch from chemicals at work. ET had bad knee from early age.	Collected milk from local farm for new babies in the family. Children had only second hand clothes and shoes. All the children helped in the house. Father decorated the house. Father paid into insurance. Welfare nurse visited family. Lengthy but confusing responses. Detail: siblings; soup kitchens during 1926 strike; knitting; teachers; doctors; food; washing arrangements; charity clothes; funerals; confinement; National Children's Homes.	Interviewee's home	2	Stoke-on-Trent	8	18.05.1971; 22.05.1971	Carol Holder	 	C707/228/1-3	1	1	64	2000int228	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	3	Open reel	1	2	1	1
