 the Ragged School Union. Stopped when 12 years old.	Mother kept a cat. Father played cards with his brothers. Children had birthday parties and presents. Children played indoor games together. Roller skates. Children were given pocket money. Visited cinema. Sunday School outing for two weeks to Chelmsford one year, and outings to Southwark park. Belonged to the Boy Scouts. Played football and cricket for West Square Senior School, and later in the Army.	Father committed suicide in 1911. Had been a heavy drinker. Midwife neighbour attended mother in confinement. Invalided 1917, damaged eyes. Lost sight in left eye and badly damaged the right.	Clothes and shoes were bought at Bermondsey market. Father mended family's shoes. One of the daughters always stayed at home to help. CDJ had to clean cutlery. After father's death, mother went to Town Hall for financial help. Mother and father were quite well educated. Wife lived in same building as CDJ's family. Detail: Red Elephant Gang; flat dwellers; CDJ's children's careers; apprenticeship in fancy leather goods; work as car man; meeting wife; siblings' careers and marriage.	Interviewee's home	3	London	3	~	~	 	C707/229/1-2	1	1	79	2000int229	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	2	Open reel	1	2	1	1
