e. Cycled to work and as his hobby. Father took The Star. Christmas was very modes. Family belonged to the Chase Library. Family had outings to Southend on privilege railway tickets. Sunday School outings to Rushted Woods, Ashstead Woods, Worthing. Summer days were spent in Southwark Park. Children played five stones (alley gobs). At school enjoyed Friday afternoons, knitting and being read to. Went to penny pictures, Elephant and Castle theatre. Visited relations who had a piano for musical evenings and to play card games. Later played tennis. Went to London theatres and dancing with boyfriend.	Was one of the first children to have trial vaccination: lost her hair and had severe eczema.	Children had to help in the house and take care of each other from an early age. Father was an amateur boxer before marriage. Father went to the pub every night. Father had better food than the rest of the family. Father cycled with his brother. Washing was done at public baths in Camberwell. Children were given free shoes from school. Grandmother made dolls out of black stockings for the children. Mother was bad-tempered and a prude, showed affection only while children were babies. Mother belonged to slate club. Detail: family food; family relationships; punishment; school; social class; police; railway; teenage.	Interviewee's home	3	London	3	27.05.1971	~	 	C707/230/1-2	1	1	57	2000int230	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/interview.asp?id=230.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	2	Open reel	1	2	1	1
