joined the Church Guild.	Father took mother and LMC with him when he had a country delivery. Parents had many friends. Had Sunday evening sing-songs and played draughts. Family owned gramophone after WW!. Family owned books by Dickens, H G Wells and Shakespeare. Father went on a walking holiday with his brother. Went to see plays at the Old Vic. Father sometimes took LMC to the music hall. LMC collected stamps, and did needlework and embroidery.. Later went to Gaiety. Sunday school outings to Southend and Riddlesdown. Belonged to the Girl Guides. Later Workers’ Educational Association outings and church socials.	Twins died in infancy. Had diphtheria and scarlet fever. Spent time in the isolation hospital at Shooters Hill.	Was fearful of water and horses. Father gave children nicknames. Was kept at home to help when another sibling born. Mother made girls' clothes. Mother decorated the home. Father took LMC out on Sundays to the Coliseum. Mother grew flowers and a grapevine in her front garden, and made wine from the grapes Mother kept two cats. Christmas was celebrated modestly. Father paraded the company's horses at the Horse Parade in Regents Park. Children were not allowed to play in the street. LMC belonged to Hearts of Oak insurance. Anecdotes. Detail: family history; relations; Dr Alfred Salter; social class; muffin man; lavender girl; cat meat woman; boyfriends and girlfriends; siblings' jobs.	Interviewee's home	3	Brightlingsea	2	18.05.1971	Paul Thompson	 	C707/235/1-5	1	1	120	2000int235	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	5	Open reel	1	2	1	1
