sional day trips to the seaside. Father and WB were interested in sports, particularly running, and went to Trent Bridge. Bank Holidays fairs. Enjoyed drawing and painting; visited stately homes and galleries. Sketched while in the Army in France. Walked and went blackberry picking with friends from school. Skated on frozen Grantham Canal. Visited and were visited by friends and relations. Family went to pantomime once a year at Theatre Royal. Attended concerts at Daybrook chapel. Stayed for weekends with cousin in Nottingham. Tony and trap rides with friends to Nottingham, Hestleford or Biskiton. Went bike riding after marriage. WB's children were given birthday fuddles; Christmas made special. Had books and newspapers in the home. On Bank Holidays walked to Langley or Oxon.	Father died of typhoid fever and perforated bowels in 1897.	Father tailored sons' suits. Boots were bought in Nottingham once a year. Father helped in the home and with children. Parents told stories and read to the children. Ran errands to Nottingham for mother. Parents grew vegetables, blackcurrants and apples, and kept hens. Mother made jams, herb beer and herbal medicines. WB helped in the garden. Parents paid into insurance and medical aid (Dr Stenhouse). Mother had help from Guardians. After marriage, helped at home and with his children. Paid into National Deposit to until aged 70 years. Wife paid into insurance. Detail: local farms and surroundings; children's games: Lurky and Stalky, Stick and Billet; social class; local manufacturers and traders; Daybrook church; Sammy Robinson; stoving; family meals; home-made medicines; punishment; footpaths; floods; clog skates.	Interviewee's home	2	Nottingham	1	02.06.1971; 09.06.1971; 16.06.19; 30.06.1971	Jean Jacobs	 	C707/237/1-4	1	1	97	2000int237	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	4	Open reel	1	2	1	1
