on Moss. Boxed at a boys club in Palace Street, run by Canon Green. GS had his own pony and trap. Went to the cinema and Grand Theatre, and on monkey run, with friends.	~	Policeman was charged with murder.	Interviewee's home	3	Salford	5	11.02.1970; 12.02.1970; 17.02.1970	Anne Burke	 	C707/258/1-2	1	1	62	2000int258	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	2	Open reel	1	2	1	1
259	1	Mr	James	Watson	W	1890	1	4	0	5	2	996	0	1860	999	999	999	1	97	97	5	1	0	1	9	9	9	9	1	0	0	9	9	9	1	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	5	9	9	9	9	5	5	2	3	9	1	1	9	9	2	2	99	99	99	9	9	4	4	2	13	2	9	99	9	9	2	13	2	0	9	9	4	9	1	3	7	7	9	999	999	999	2	2	4	6	6	9	7	9	9	259	132	132	999	9	2	9	2	9	2	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	99	99	1	5	2	9	1	0	1	1	3	2	4	999	9	9	2	99	2	4	999	9	9	2	99	7	9	999	9	9	2	99	259	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	99	99	99	9	9	9	9	9	Saddlers business	B	2	2	4	Family, housing, discipline, education, work, leisure, religion, politics, marriage, childbirth and local community.	Born in Lauder, Berwick. Move to Kirkwood. Happy, close family. Six siblings. JW had a twin brother.	Never married. Lived with an aunt in Edgarhope, then lived with older brother.	Kirkwind home, owned, had six rooms, with large garret, and indoor lavatory. Grandmother lived with the family.	Started at infant school age five years. Moved school aged 11 years. Left age 15 years. Parents were interested in school work.	Father was a plumber and slater. Had been a baker. Was also a barber and chimney sweep.	Worked in a bakers before and after school, and on Saturdays. Became an apprentice saddler after leaving school. Was in the Army during World War I. Was head observer in the Royal Observer Corp. After the war, did a number of odd jobs before returning to saddling. Served a four year apprenticeship. Bought the business 1928 or 1930. Brother had owned a gasworks which JW took over after his brother died and until Nationalisation. Was a town councillor and provost, and Elder in the Church.	Parents and JW were Liberal.	Family went to (possibly Free) church. Children attended Sunday School, Bible class and Welfare of Youth.	Father told children stories. Children were given a small present and special tea for birthdays. Family celebrated Christmas, New Year and Guising. Sister played the organ; one brother was a good singer and dancer. Father was a singer; JW was a singer and played the violin. Father and mother read. The Bible was read out loud on Sunday evenings. JW and twin spent time in the holidays with an aunt in Edinburgh. Boys caught fish with their hands, played football. As a young man, played golf, football and went walking.	~	The garret room had a window overlooking the cemetery. Possible look-out for body stealers. Uncle was a tailor. Father was a Free Mason. Mother baked bread and made jam. Parents grew vegetables and fruit, also kept hens and horses. Father sometime bought a pig and cured it. Bought cigarettes or toffee with pocket money. Lived in close and friendly community. Brother's widow became JW's housekeeper. Detail: family's food; local community; changes in Lauder and Lauder community.	Interviewee's home	2	Lauder	11	??.08.1971; ??.11.1971	George Megahy	 	C707/259/1-2	1	1	35	2000int259	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	2	Open reel	1	2	1	1
