oliday with parents to Rothesay and Dunoon. Sunday school outings to Bury, Muirend, Crookston Castle, or Calhouston. Enjoyed walking and boxing. Watched football on Saturdays.	Father died of a heart attack in 1890.	Children's clothes were bought in Rattan Road. Mother knitted. Children were given Gregory's mixture. Royal Air Force smoked from the age of 14 years. Father was a Freemason. Royal Air Force joined the Freemasons in 1925. Detail: father's funeral; social class; religion; work.	Interviewee's home	3	Glasgow	11	25.10.1971; 29.10.1971	Barbara Stilwell	 	C707/260/1-2	1	1	68	2000int260	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	2	Open reel	1	2	1	1
261	1	Mrs	Caroline Bessie	Costello	C	1893	2	4	8	1	1	1919	3	999	999	999	999	1	97	97	1	1	0	0	3	3	0	0	0	0	0	0	0	0	1	5	5	5	5	4	4	4	5	5	9	9	5	5	5	1	9	9	4	9	5	9	3	1	99	99	99	5	6	4	4	3	1	1	9	99	9	9	3	1	1	0	9	9	5	9	4	0	5	0	3	1	3	4	1	1	7	3	4	3	9	9	3	261	11	11	999	1	2	1	2	1	3	7	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	99	99	4	2	2	9	0	4	0	2	2	2	4	999	9	9	2	11	7	9	999	9	9	2	99	5	3	999	9	9	2	99	261	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	99	99	99	9	9	9	9	9	Housework	G	97	7	9	Family, housing, discipline, education, work, leisure, religion, politics, marriage, childbirth and local community.	Born in the Old Kent Road, London. Eldest child of 9 siblings.	Married in 1919. Three children.	Several moves. Rented Guy's houses. House in Kipling Street, Bermondsey had six rooms. Lived in a nurses' home.	Started Laxton Street Board School aged five years. Left aged 14 years. Took first-aid at evening classes.	Father was a blacksmith hammer man and labourer. Often out of work. Mother sewed boxes at Bankside. Later made Smiths crisps.	Went into service in Balham on leaving school. Stayed for five years. Was briefly in service in Brixton. Worked for a German family, bakers, who were interned. Then became a waitress at ABC for a few weeks. Made gas masks then worked at the Woolwich Arsenal tidying cordite for shell cases during World War I. Became overlooker at the Woolwich Arsenal. After World War I, became a hospital housemaid. Belonged to the Public Employees Union.	Father was Labour. CBC was Labour.	Attended Church of England Sunday school and Methodist chapel for magic lantern shows and sing-songs in the afternoons. Attended Band of Hope.	Mother went out with friends from work. Children went with parents to Gatties and the Old Rotherhithe Hippodrome music hall. Belonged to Girls Friendly Society. Went to Southend and Leigh-on-Sea with the society. Enjoyed reading. While a schoolgirl, went to a farm in Warwickshire through Country Holiday Fund which Mother paid into. Children received small presents for birthdays if there was money. Christmas was celebrated. Father played, sang and danced with the children. Father was interested in local history and read Dickens. Belonged to the Library. Parents enjoyed reading. Family had outings to Greenwich Park.	Mother had an infectious skin disease. Many siblings died in infancy. CBC had tonsillitis.	Father drank. Father helped in the home when he was out of work. Father fattened fowl for Christmas, and kept rabbits. Children had school dinners. Went to Gays Hospital Causality when ill. Family had help from the Guardians. School dinners were supplied by Shaftesbury Hall. Father signed for National Service but was too old. Exception interview. Many anecdotes. Detail: housing; welfare; church/mission hall visitor; social class; building of Thames Embankment; work in service; false birth certificates; employer’s references.	Interviewee's home	3	London	3	29.07.1971; 05.08.1971	Trevor Lummis	 	C707/261/1-3	1	1	89	2000int261	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	3	Open reel	1	2	1	1
262	1	Mr	Frederick Henry	Taylor	T	1879	1	4	0	5	1	1911	2	1851	1934	1853	1936	1	97	97	2	3	1	0	9	9	9	9	1	0	0	9	9	9	4	4	4	5	4	4	4	4	4	4	4	5	9	5	5	1	1	1	2	1	9	9	3	2	2	99	99	4	3	4	4	2	6	3	3	6	3	0	3	6	3	0	9	9	1	9	2	0	7	7	9	999	999	999	2	2	4	9	3	3	2	2	5	262	63	63	63	2	2	2	2	2	1	7	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	9	99	99	2	2	2	9	1	4	1	1	4	5	2	999	9	9	9	99	5	9	999	9	9	9	99	5	2	999	9	9	9	99	262	2	1	9	9	5	5	9	9	9	9	9	9	3	3	0	0	0	0	0	0	1	9	9	1	9	9	9	2	2	4	99	99	1	1	1	1	9	Senior clerical work	C	3	3	4	Family, housing, discipline, education, work, leisure, religion, politics, marriage, childbirth and local community.	Born in Hanley, Staffordshire. Fifth child of 7 siblings: 4 brothers, 2 sisters. Maternal grandmother lived with the family. Affectionate mother.	Married in 1911. One son, one daughter.	Lived in the same three bed roomed house for two six years. House was bought on a mortgage. Then lived with brothers and sisters, numerous changes of address, until marriage. Lived in Birches Head to 1939 in two bed roomed house with bathroom. Owned the house.	Started at Charles Street Wesleyan school aged four years. Continued at same school until ex-seven, age 13 years. Took shorthand and French, pottery and chemistry at evening classes.	Father was an invoice clerk for Calder and Wilks.	Worked for two weeks for a shipping agents aged 12 years. First full-time job was with Wooliscrofts in the cashiers office, worked as an office boy and in invoicing. Stayed for 19-20 years until was given the sack because the company could not pay his wage. From 1911 worked for Johnson Brothers potters, gilding and lithography. During World War I, did numerous jobs, as required by manpower shortages, including lodge man at Hanley Pottery, mixing glazing, paying wages, account and buying. Most years were spent in the cashiers office. Left in 1944. Wife at been at Reading University, was a teacher before marriage.	Family voted Liberal. FHT was Liberal, later Labour.	Family attended Bethesda Methodist Chapel. Children went to Sunday school, Christian Endeavour and Bible classes. Sunday school had a library. FHT became a Sunday school teacher.	Family took holidays in Aberystwyth once a year. Sunday school outings to Stoke Fields. Family went to the Victoria Hall. Children were given birthday presents. Christmas was celebrated. Family owned a piano. One brother played the violin. Family had sing-songs. Parents played games, including chess, with the children. Parents took the Christian Herald and the Sentinel. Family visited relations. Outings to Trentham Park. Enjoyed cycling and walking. Collected coins and stamps. When older, belonged to the YMCA cycling club. Had friends from childhood. After marriage: Played with his children. Celebrated birthdays and Christmas. Wife played the piano. Many books in the home. Belonged to Hanley Library. Took the Christian Herald and The Sentinel. Family went on rambles to Badley Edge. Visited relatives. Took holidays on the Isle of Man, Tenby, Swansea, and other named places. FHT stayed for one week, the family stayed on for a further week.	FHT's first child was stillborn after wife had a fall.	Father had been in the Voluntary Army Service before marriage. Mother had help in the house. Children had tasks. Mother prepared bread dough which was baked at baker. Mother made mincemeat and an egg pick-me-up. Parents were members of a working men's club. Parents moved to Penzance to benefit mother's health. FHT wrote poetry as a young man. Bought and sold houses. After marriage: FHT helped with housework and children. Wife was in the Women's Voluntary, belonged to the Women's Institute. Comprehensive interview. Detail: family friends; social evenings; employers; mentions many names; chapel; family members.	Interviewee's home	2	Stoke-on-Trent	8	??.05.1971	Carroll Hill	 	C707/262/1-3	1	1	81	2000int262	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/notranscript.asp	http://www.esds.ac.uk/qualidata/online/explore/nomaterial.asp	3	Open reel	1	2	1	1
